[doctor] any depression or excessive worrying or mood swings?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk about your past medical history. you've had urinary incontinence, adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, and cognitive impairment. so, let's talk about your results. so, your ct scan from october 15th, 2020 shows that you have a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that's flat with the span of 10 mm. by my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. this was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. there is also substantial small vessel ischemic change. so, i think you've made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since your shunt surgery. there is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. i do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the ct scan. so, i think you'll be okay. so, i want to talk to you about your driving. i know you've been asking about that. unfortunately, i think it's unlikely that your speed of movement will improve to a level that you'll be able to pass a driver's safety evaluation. however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they are able to pass such a test. i would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if i believe you're likely to pass it. however, i think it is exceeding unlikely that you have the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. i also think it's quite likely you will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test. so, we'll follow you up in three months. i want to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt. any questions?
