dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, miss sullivan. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing well. thank you.
[doctor] good. so, i see you're here today for some right elbow pain. can you tell me a little bit about that?
[patient] yes, my elbow has been hurting for about 4 days now. i have no idea what caused it.
[doctor] okay. and you said you've been working with a pottery wheel?
[patient] yes, i have been working with a pottery wheel to make a bowl for my son. it's a big one.
[doctor] okay. and when did you start using the pottery wheel?
[patient] i think it was about 4 days ago.
[doctor] okay. and you said you've been working with it for the last 4 days?
[patient] yes, i have.
[doctor] okay. and you said you've been having pain in your elbow. does it hurt anywhere else?
[patient] it hurts in the medial aspect of my elbow. and it radiates down my arm.
[doctor] okay. and how would you rate the pain?
[patient] it's about a 7 out of 10.
[doctor] okay. and does it wake you up at night?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. and do you use any ice or ibuprofen?
[patient] no, i haven't tried that.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any previous elbow injuries?
[patient] no, none that i can think of.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your history. you have hypertension. how have you been managing that?
[patient] i've been checking my blood pressure regularly. it's been in the 110s to 130s.
[doctor] okay. and are you taking your lisinopril?
[patient] yes, i'm taking 10 mg daily. i haven't missed any doses.
[doctor] okay. and you also have diabetes type 2. how have you been managing that?
[patient] i take metformin 500 mg twice a day. sometimes i forget to take it.
[doctor] okay. and have you been checking your blood sugars regularly?
[patient] yes, i check them.
[doctor] okay. and what are they running?
[patient] they've been ranging from 150 to 170.
[doctor] okay. and how often do you exercise?
[patient] i walk in the morning and evening. i walk about 1 to 2 miles a day.
[doctor] okay. and do you have a sweet tooth?
[patient] yes, i do. i love baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your medical history. have you had any other surgeries or illnesses?
[patient] no, not that i can think of.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other symptoms? any other pain or discomfort?
[patient] no, just my elbow.
[doctor] okay. all right. let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'm going to listen to your heart and lungs. your heart sounds good. your lungs are clear. i'm not hearing any wheezes or crackles. i'm going to check your blood pressure. it's 120 over 70. your pulse is good. your respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute. your oxygen saturation is 100% on room air. your body temperature is 97.9. and your pain level is a 7 out of 10 at its worst.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. so, based on your symptoms and your exam, i think you have medial epicondylitis, which is a common condition that causes pain on the inside of the elbow. it's often caused by overuse or injury to the tendons in the arm. i'm going to order an x-ray of your elbow, but i don't think you have a fracture. based on your exam, i think you do have some tendinitis. i'm going to recommend that you wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide some support. i want you to ice your elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. you can take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. and i want you to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] i know it's frustrating, but i think this will help it heal faster. do you have any questions about that?
[patient] no, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. i also want you to check your blood sugars every day. i'm going to increase your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. it's important to take your medication regularly. and i'm going to order a hemoglobin a1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want you to continue taking your lisinopril 10 mg. continue with your walking routine. and i'm also going to refer you to diabetic counseling for more information on how to manage your diabetes.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. do you have any other questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay. you'll follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin a1c test and to discuss your progress. do you have any other questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay. well, i'll see you in 2 weeks. take care.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
