[doctor] okay. and do you have any other hobbies or activities that you do outside of work that involve lifting or bending?
[patient] not really.
[doctor] okay. okay. so let's go ahead and do a physical exam. i'm just going to look in your eyes, and i'm not seeing any redness, discharge, or icterus. i'm going to listen to your heart and lungs and everything sounds good there. i'm going to listen to your ears and everything sounds good there. and i'm going to check your back. so i'm going to press on your back here, and i'm going to feel around. i'm not feeling any pain when i press on your upper back. but when i press on the middle of your back, especially on the left side, i do feel some pain. i'm not feeling any pain when i press on the right side. and i'm going to check your legs. so i'm going to press down on your legs and i'm feeling some pain when i do this on the right leg. i'm feeling some pain on the left leg as well. i'm going to check your straight leg raise. so i'm going to lift your leg up and i'm feeling some pain when i do this on the right leg. and i'm feeling some pain on the left leg as well. so, i'm going to order an x-ray of your back, and we'll get that done today. i'm also going to refer you to physical therapy. physical therapy can help with the pain, but it can also help strengthen some of the muscles in your back. and i want to make sure you're careful when you're taking your medication. you're taking ibuprofen, and that's okay, but if you're doing any strenuous or hazardous activities like driving, you need to be careful. i'm also going to give you some flexeril. this is a muscle relaxer. take 400 milligrams up to three times a day. if you need something for severe pain, you can take tylenol. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm also going to order a hemoglobin a1c. so, i want you to check your blood sugar in the morning and log that information for me. i know you have diabetes. so, let's talk a little bit about your diabetes. how are you doing with that?
[patient] i've been doing okay.
[doctor] okay. have you been checking your blood sugar?
[patient] yes, i have been.
[doctor] okay. and how often are you checking it?
[patient] i've been checking it every morning.
[doctor] okay. and have you been keeping a log of those numbers?
[patient] yes, i have been.
[doctor] okay. and how has your hemoglobin a1c been?
[patient] i think it was like 7.1 last time.
[doctor] okay. okay. i'm going to order a hemoglobin a1c today. so, let's talk a little bit about diet. are you watching what you eat?
[patient] yeah, i've been trying to.
[doctor] okay. are you avoiding foods that are high in sugar?
[patient] yeah, i've been trying.
[doctor] okay. and are you doing any exercise?
[patient] yeah, i've been trying to walk a little bit.
[doctor] okay. great. okay. so, i want you to try to avoid foods high in sugar and try to exercise a little bit more. i know you have diabetes. so, let's go over the plan. so, i'm going to order an x-ray of your back. i'm going to refer you to physical therapy. i'm going to give you some flexeril, and i want you to take 400 milligrams up to three times a day. if you need something for severe pain, you can take tylenol. i'm also going to order a hemoglobin a1c, and i want you to check your blood sugar in the morning and log that information for me. does that sound okay?
[patient] yeah, that sounds fine.
[doctor] okay. do you have any questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay. great. all right, i'll see you in the next room to get the x-ray.
