dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, alexis, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm here for the pain in my right hand.
[doctor] okay. so, tell me about this pain in your right hand.
[patient] so it's been going on for about six months now. i've been having some numbness and tingling in my first, second, and third fingers on my right hand. it's been constant for the past month or so. and it's really bad when i'm typing or using the mouse. i do that all day at work.
[doctor] okay. have you had any sleep disturbance?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been doing for the pain?
[patient] i've just been resting and taking ibuprofen.
[doctor] okay. and has that been helpful?
[patient] yeah, it's been helpful, but it's not really going away.
[doctor] okay. so, do you have any other symptoms like pain in your neck or your shoulders or anything like that?
[patient] no, it's just my hand.
[doctor] okay. have you had any trauma to your hand or anything like that?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. alright. so, let's go ahead and do a physical exam. so, i'm just going to check your hand here. so, i'm going to press on your wrist. does that hurt?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and then i'm going to press on your fingers here. does that hurt?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to press on your thumb. does that hurt?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and then i'm just going to check your grip strength. can you squeeze my hand for me?
[patient] sure.
[doctor] okay. so, let me just feel your grip strength here. so, your grip strength is a little weaker on the right compared to the left. and then i'm going to do a little test called the tinnel's test. i'm just going to press on the nerve here at the wrist. and then i'm going to press on your fingers here. does that hurt?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. so, all of those tests are positive. so, i think what's going on is that you have a condition called carpal tunnel syndrome. so, the carpal tunnel is a tunnel in the wrist. and there's a nerve that runs through that tunnel. and what's happening is that the nerve is getting pinched, which is causing the numbness and tingling and the pain in your hand. and the pain is worse when you're using your hand, like when you're typing or using the mouse. so, i'm going to prescribe you some ibuprofen. i'm going to increase the dose to 600 milligrams four times a day. and i also want you to wear a wrist splint at night. this will help to keep your wrist in a neutral position. and hopefully, that will help to alleviate the symptoms. i'm also going to order an emg nerve conduction study. this is a test that will help us to see how well the nerve is functioning. and if the results show that the nerve is still being pinched, we can discuss other treatment options such as physical therapy or surgery.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] does that sound good?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] alright. do you have any other questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] alright. alright, well, thank you so much for coming in today. i'll have my nurse come in and get you checked out.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
