dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi angela, how are you today?
[patient] i'm doing okay, doctor. i've been having a lot of pain in my left knee.
[doctor] okay, i see. so, tell me a little bit more about your knee pain. when did it start?
[patient] it started about a week ago.
[doctor] okay. what happened?
[patient] i was running around with my niece and i fell.
[doctor] okay. did you hear a pop?
[patient] yes, i did.
[doctor] okay. and you think you twisted your knee?
[patient] yes, i think i did.
[doctor] okay. and what has been happening since then?
[patient] i've been limping.
[doctor] okay. and where exactly is the pain located?
[patient] it's on the inside and the outside of my knee.
[doctor] okay. and how would you rate the pain level?
[patient] it's about a 4 out of 10.
[doctor] okay. and is it constant or does it come and go?
[patient] it's constant.
[doctor] okay. and what has been helping with the pain?
[patient] i've been taking tylenol.
[doctor] okay. and has that been helping?
[patient] it helps a little bit, but it's not much.
[doctor] okay. and have you been wrapping your knee with any tape?
[patient] yes, i've been wrapping it with kinesiology tape.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to do anything that makes the pain worse?
[patient] i've been trying to walk on it, but it hurts.
[doctor] okay. and what about ibuprofen? i know you said you have an ulcer.
[patient] i'm not supposed to take it.
[doctor] okay. so, you're just taking tylenol. have you been able to do anything that makes it feel better?
[patient] just wrapping it with the tape helps a little bit.
[doctor] okay. alright. well, let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'm gon na push on your knee now. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, it hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na bend your knee. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na straighten it out. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. i'm gon na push on the inside of your knee. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na push on the outside of your knee. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. alright, let's go ahead and get some x-rays of your knee. we'll take a look at those and see what's going on. i'm gon na go ahead and order some x-rays of your knee. we'll get those taken and then we can go ahead and talk about what's going on with you.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] alright. so, i've reviewed the x-rays of your knee and everything looks good. there's no fractures or dislocations. there's no signs of a tear. i think you probably strained your mcl.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, what i'm gon na do is put you in a knee immobilizer to give you a little bit more support for the next week. i'm gon na prescribe you some tramadol 50 milligrams to help with the pain. you can take tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. i'm also gon na have you ice your knee three to four times a day for twenty to thirty minutes at a time. i want you to refrain from running for the next three weeks.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] if your pain does not improve with the immobilizer, i'm gon na have to recommend getting an mri.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] does that sound okay with you?
[patient] yes, that sounds good.
[doctor] alright. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay. great. well, i'm gon na go ahead and get you set up with the immobilizer and the tramadol. we'll get you all fixed up and you'll be back running in no time.
[patient] okay. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
