dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Good morning, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, but I've been having some pain in my elbow.

[doctor]: I see. Where exactly is the pain?

[patient]: It's in my left elbow. It's been really bothering me.

[doctor]: I understand. How long has this pain been going on?

[patient]: It started about a week ago. It's been pretty constant.

[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Can you tell me more about the pain? Is it constant or does it come and go?

[patient]: It's pretty constant. It's stiff and painful.

[doctor]: Okay. Does the pain change when you move your elbow?

[patient]: Yes, it gets worse when I flex or extend it. It's really hard to do either.

[doctor]: I see. Have you noticed any swelling in the area?

[patient]: Yes, there is some swelling on the back of my elbow.

[doctor]: That's interesting. Are you right- or left-handed?

[patient]: I'm right-handed.

[doctor]: Alright. How has the pain affected your daily activities?

[patient]: It's hard to type because of the pain in my left elbow.

[doctor]: Hmm, that can be frustrating. Have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your hands?

[patient]: No, I haven't.

[doctor]: Good. How about weakness in your arm?

[patient]: I do feel a little weak, but not too much.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you had any fever or chills?

[patient]: No, I haven't.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's talk about your medical history. You mentioned you have epilepsy. How's that been?

[patient]: I haven't had any seizures recently. My last one was about a year ago.

[doctor]: Good to hear that. You're still taking Keppra?

[patient]: Yes, I am.

[doctor]: Great. How about depression? You mentioned you're seeing a therapist.

[patient]: Yes, I started therapy last year and it's been helpful. I've been trying to avoid medication for it.

[doctor]: Okay. How have you been feeling about your depression lately?

[patient]: It's still there, but I've been trying to manage it with therapy.

[doctor]: I see. Have you had any suicidal or homicidal thoughts?

[patient]: No, I haven't.

[doctor]: Good to hear that. You also have peptic ulcer disease. How's that been?

[patient]: I had a lot of stress last year and I had an endoscopy which showed gastritis. I was diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started taking Protonix.

[doctor]: I see. How have you been doing with the Protonix?

[patient]: Everything has been good since the procedure.

[doctor]: That's great to hear. Any chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting?

[patient]: No, none of those.

[doctor]: Hmm, any nasal congestion?

[patient]: Yes, I have a little bit of medial nasal congestion from allergies.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's do a quick review of systems. How's your ear, nose, and throat been?

[patient]: My nose is congested from allergies.

[doctor]: Okay. How about your cardiovascular system?

[patient]: No chest pain or shortness of breath.

[doctor]: Good. Respiratory system?

[patient]: No shortness of breath.

[doctor]: Alright. Musculoskeletal system?

[patient]: My left elbow is really bothering me.

[doctor]: I see. Any psychiatric symptoms?

[patient]: I have depression.

[doctor]: Okay. Let's do a physical exam. I'll start with your left elbow. I see some erythema and edema. There's pain when I palpate the olecranon. It's warm to the touch, and there's pain with flexion and extension. I can feel your radial pulse.

[patient]: Oh, that sounds painful.

[doctor]: It is. I'll order an x-ray of your left arm to rule out any fracture. The results came back and there's no evidence of an elbow fracture.

[patient]: Good to hear that.

[doctor]: I also had you do an EGD, an endoscopy, which showed an episode of gastritis. We'll continue the Protonix for that.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, I believe you have left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma.

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: It's inflammation in the bursa, the fluid-filled sac, on the back of your elbow. It's likely caused by repetitive bending and flexing of your elbow at work.

[patient]: Ah, that makes sense.

[doctor]: I'll start you on meloxicam 15 mg once daily to help with the pain and inflammation.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I also encourage you to use a pillow to rest your arm on and to ice the area.

[patient]: I can do that.

[doctor]: Your epilepsy is under control with Keppra. Let's continue that at 500 mg twice a day. I'll refill it for you today.

[patient]: Great, thank you.

[doctor]: Your depression is managed with therapy, and we'll start you on Lexapro 10 mg once daily.

[patient]: Okay, I'll start that.

[doctor]: Continue the Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals for your peptic ulcer disease.

[patient]: Alright, I'll keep doing that.

[doctor]: You understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan?

[patient]: Yes, I do.

[doctor]: Good. We'll monitor your progress and adjust the treatment as needed. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor]: Alright, we'll see you in six weeks for a follow-up. Take care.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
