dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi Emily, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm good, thanks. I'm here for my annual follow-up for my asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy.

[doctor]: Great, let's go over your history. How has your asthma been?

[patient]: It's been okay. I've been using Wixela, but I've been reducing my dose to one puff three to four times a week. I feel it's working well.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. You've been switched to Wixela from Symbicort due to insurance changes. How has the new medication been working for you?

[patient]: It's been working well. I use it once a day, three to four times a week, and twice a day on other days.

[doctor]: Have you had any urgent care or emergency room visits for respiratory issues in the past year?

[patient]: No, I haven't.

[doctor]: And have you needed prednisone for breathing issues in the past year?

[patient]: No, I haven't.

[doctor]: Great. You mentioned you're trying to join the military and they require a certain fitness level without using an inhaler. How's your progress been?

[patient]: I'm still working on it. I'm undergoing exertional testing to see how I can do without my inhaler. They need to see if I can meet the physical fitness requirements.

[doctor]: That's important. You've been reducing your Wixela dose to meet these requirements. How's that going?

[patient]: It's going well. I've been using it only three to four times a week.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. You mentioned you've been smoking and vaping, but you're trying to quit. How's that going?

[patient]: Yeah, I'm trying to cut down. I've reduced my use significantly.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you used your nebulizer recently?

[patient]: No, I haven't. I only use it when I have an upper respiratory infection or as a child a few times.

[doctor]: Alright. You mentioned you use Zyrtec during the spring and fall, but not consistently during the summer. How often do you use it now?

[patient]: I use it three days a week when I work outside. It works well to control my symptoms.

[doctor]: That's good. You've had an incident with peanuts in the past. Can you tell me about that?

[patient]: Yes, I was cleaning an Enterprise rental car that had a lot of peanuts in it. I developed contact hives when the peanuts touched my skin.

[doctor]: Oh, I see. Have you had any other accidental exposures to peanuts since then?

[patient]: No, I haven't. I wear long sleeves and change my gloves between each cleaning. I think it's likely that I've been in contact with cars that had peanuts, but I haven't touched them directly.

[doctor]: That's a good strategy. You also avoid tree nuts, right?

[patient]: Yes, I do. I tried almonds and they didn't cause any issues, but I still avoid all tree nuts.

[doctor]: Alright. You have an EpiPen with you, right?

[patient]: Yes, I always keep it with me.

[doctor]: Great. Let's go over your medical history. You have asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergies to peanuts and tree nuts. Is that correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: You're currently taking Wixela Inhub 250 mcg-50 mcg/dose powder for inhalation, 1 puff twice daily in the morning and evening. You also take Proair HFA as needed and an EpiPen as needed. Is that correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's correct.

[doctor]: Your Allergy Test results show you're sensitive to peanuts. Have you had any other reactions to any medications?

[patient]: Yes, I had a seizure-like reaction to Montelukast sodium.

[doctor]: Okay. Your Asthma Control Test score is 21. That's a good score. Your current food skin test sensitivities include peanuts. How do you feel about your current asthma management?

[patient]: I feel it's under control.

[doctor]: That's great. Based on your current ACT score and your spirometry test, it looks like your asthma is well-controlled. You're doing a good job managing it. We'll continue to monitor it.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I'll prescribe you Alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puffs twice daily. You can start this immediately. Keep the Wixela in case the Alvesco doesn't work well. You can continue using your albuterol as needed.

[patient]: Okay, I'll start the Alvesco.

[doctor]: You'll also continue using Zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed. For your peanut allergy, I'll refill your EpiPen today. It expires annually, so make sure to keep it updated.

[patient]: Thank you.

[doctor]: Great. Let's review the plan. You'll start Alvesco and keep Wixela as a backup. You'll continue your albuterol as needed. You'll also keep using Zyrtec as needed. For your peanut allergy, you'll continue to avoid exposure and keep your EpiPen with you. You'll return in four months to repeat the ACT and spirometry testing.

[patient]: Okay, I understand. Thank you.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think I'm good.

[doctor]: Alright. We'll see you in four months. Take care and good luck with your military endeavors.

[patient]: Thank you. I'll see you then.

[doctor]: Bye, Emily. }}<|eot_id|>
