dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing alright, I guess. I've been having some issues with my stomach again.

[doctor] I see. Let's discuss your symptoms. You've been diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) before. How have your symptoms been since your last visit in August 2019?

[patient] They've been pretty bad. I've been having a lot of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.

[doctor] I see. Did you follow the treatment plan we discussed previously?

[patient] Yes, I did. I started taking Bentyl as prescribed, and I also started a low dose fiber supplement and probiotics.

[doctor] Good to hear. Did you stop taking Reglan as instructed?

[patient] Yes, I stopped Reglan and started taking Zofran as needed for nausea.

[doctor] Alright. How has your experience been with the Bentyl?

[patient] It's been okay, but I've still been having some abdominal pain and cramps.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. Have you noticed any specific foods or activities that trigger your symptoms?

[patient] Well, I had shrimp recently and it really upset my stomach. I also get diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo, but not fried chicken. And when I drink, I have diarrhea too.

[doctor] Okay, let's break that down. You said shrimp caused upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating. And you get diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo. That's interesting. Have you noticed any other symptoms like that?

[patient] Yeah, when I eat chicken alfredo, I get diarrhea, but not fried chicken. And I have 2-3 formed stools a day.

[doctor] Alright, I see. It sounds like you're having a bit of a mix of symptoms. Let's talk about your diet. Do you avoid certain foods or drinks that trigger your symptoms?

[patient] I try to avoid heavy foods, but I ate fried chicken today and didn't have any symptoms. I've also stopped drinking soda, which has been helpful.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Have you noticed any other changes in your symptoms since we last spoke?

[patient] Yes, I've had some sharp pains and cramps, especially on Mondays through Thursdays.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. Have you been experiencing any stress or anxiety lately?

[patient] Yes, I've been really stressed at work and at home.

[doctor] Okay, I see. Stress can definitely exacerbate IBS symptoms. Let's talk about your sleep habits. Do you get enough sleep each night?

[patient] No, I usually get about 5-6 hours of sleep.

[doctor] Hmm, it's no surprise that your symptoms are worse given your poor sleep and eating habits. Let's discuss some lifestyle changes that might help. I'm going to start you on a neuromodulator, Elavil, 25 mg at bedtime. This can help manage your IBS symptoms.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] Also, I want you to take Bentyl 20 mg 30-45 minutes before eating at a restaurant. This will help with intestinal cramping. And if you need it, you can use Zofran for nausea.

[patient] Alright, I can do that.

[doctor] I also encourage you to reduce your consumption of greasy, fatty foods and alcohol. These can definitely trigger your symptoms.

[patient] Okay, I'll try to cut back.

[doctor] Great. I'd like you to send me a MyChart message in a month to let me know how you're doing and if you're having any side effects or if you need any dose adjustments. We'll also schedule a follow-up visit in a month. How does that sound?

[patient] That sounds good, thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care and see you in a month. }}<|eot_id|>
