dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you today?

[patient] Good morning, I'm doing well, thank you.

[doctor] Great to hear that. I'm Dr. Johnson, your neurosurgeon. I see you're accompanied by your daughter, Theresa. How are you feeling?

[patient] I'm feeling better, Doctor. My daughter here has been helping me a lot.

[doctor] That's wonderful. I'm glad to hear that. Let me review your file. You had shunt surgery for adult hydrocephalus back in August, is that correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] And this is your first follow-up visit since the surgery. How have you been doing since then?

[patient] Well, my walking has improved a lot. I can walk to the dining room now, which I couldn't do before.

[doctor] That's great to hear! How about your balance? Has that improved as well?

[patient] Yes, my balance has improved, but I still have some trouble walking.

[doctor] Okay, and how about your bladder control? Has there been any change there?

[patient] Initially, I had some improvement, but it's leveled off now and I still wear a diaper.

[doctor] I see. And how about your thinking? Has your cognitive function improved at all?

[patient] Yes, I can think clearer now. My daughter here says I'm easier to talk to.

[doctor] That's wonderful to hear. Your daughter, Hannah, mentioned that you're having normal conversations again. That's a great sign.

[patient] Yes, it is.

[doctor] I'm glad to hear that. Have you had any headaches or pain at the shunt site or in your abdomen?

[patient] No, no headaches and no pain.

[doctor] Alright. Let me review your medical history. You've had urinary incontinence, adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, and cognitive impairment in the past.

[patient] Yes, that's all correct.

[doctor] I see. Now, let's go over your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 124/80, heart rate is 64, respiratory rate is 18, you weigh 174 pounds, and you report no pain, which is good.

[patient] That's right.

[doctor] Excellent. Let's do a quick physical exam. Your shunt site looks good, clean, dry, and intact, and it's confirmed at a setting of 2.0.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Now, let's test your mental status. We'll check your recent and remote memory, attention span, concentration, and fund of knowledge. I'll use the MMSE test. Your score is 26/30 on spelling and 25/30 on calculations. You were able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. That's a slight improvement over your initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling.

[patient] That's good to hear.

[doctor] Yes, it is. We'll also test your gait using the Tinetti assessment tool. You were tested without an assistive device and got a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. That's a slight improvement from your initial score of 15-17/28.

[patient] That's good.

[doctor] Your cranial nerves are normal, your motor function is normal, and your coordination is slow for finger-to-nose. Now, let's review your CT scan from October 15th. It shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a flat 3rd ventricular contour and a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdural collection. The radiologist didn't notice this when they read the CT scan, but I did. There's also some small vessel ischemic change.

[patient] What does it mean?

[doctor] It means there's a small fluid collection in the right parietal region, but it's not causing any symptoms. I don't believe it's significant enough to affect your condition.

[patient] Okay, I see.

[doctor] You've made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since the surgery. Now, you asked about driving. Unfortunately, I think it's unlikely that your speed of movement will improve enough for you to pass a driver's safety evaluation. However, occasionally patients surprise me and improve enough over 9 to 12 months to pass such a test. If I think you're likely to pass, I'd be happy to recommend the test. You're also welcome to enroll in a driver's safety program without my recommendation, but I think it's unlikely you'll regain the speed of motion needed to pass.

[patient] I understand. What should I do next?

[doctor] We'll leave the shunt setting at 2.0 for another three months before considering any changes. We'll follow up in three months to reassess your condition.

[patient] Alright, I'll follow up in three months.

[doctor] Great. It's good to see you're making progress. Take care, and we'll see you in three months.

[patient] Thank you, Doctor. I'll see you then.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care. }}<|eot_id|>
