dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?

[patient] Good morning, doctor. I'm okay, but my knee has been bothering me a lot lately.

[doctor] I see. Let's go over your medical history. You were last seen by me on March 4th, 2021, and you received a steroid injection to your right knee. How has your condition been since then?

[patient] It's been pretty bad. The pain is still there, and the swelling hasn't gone away.

[doctor] I see. You mentioned the pain is a 6/10. Can you tell me more about that?

[patient] Yes, it's been 6 out of 10. It's been bothering me, especially when I stand up after sitting for a while. I have to use a cane to get my bearings.

[doctor] Hmm, I understand. The injection you received was only beneficial for a few weeks, I see. Have you noticed any whole right leg swelling since your last visit?

[patient] Yes, my whole right leg has been swollen since then.

[doctor] That's concerning. Do you wear support hose to help with the swelling?

[patient] Yes, I do wear support hose, and it helps to some extent.

[doctor] Okay, and have you attended any physical therapy sessions?

[patient] No, I haven't had the chance.

[doctor] Alright, let's do a quick physical exam. Can you please lie down and let me check your right knee?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] Great. Your lower right extremity shows swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion, and the skin is warm to touch. I'll also check for pitting edema. There is pitting edema in the lower right leg when I apply pressure.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Now, let's discuss the procedure. We'll do a fluid aspiration and steroid injection to your right knee. We'll explain the risks, benefits, and alternatives. The risks include hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia, among others.

[patient] What are the benefits?

[doctor] The benefits include temporary pain relief and reduced inflammation. The steroid injection will help reduce the swelling and ease the pain.

[patient] Alright, I understand. What are the alternatives?

[doctor] There are other treatment options, such as physical therapy and other medications. We'll discuss those later.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] We'll proceed with the procedure. We'll access your right knee through the lateral approach, and inject 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then blood-tinged fluid. We'll then inject 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure will be done under sterile conditions.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] Are you comfortable with the procedure?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] Great. The procedure is done, and you're doing well. I'll give you post-procedure instructions to follow.

[patient] Thank you.

[doctor] Your current medications include Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. You're on high-risk medications. We'll need to check your CMP levels periodically every couple of months.

[patient] Alright, I'll make sure to follow that.

[doctor] Let's discuss your knee pain. Your pain level is 6/10, and the swelling persists. We'll arrange for an x-ray of your right knee and refer you to physical therapy. We'll also discuss compression stockings, elevating your feet, and a low-salt diet. It's important to check with your primary care physician for further instructions.

[patient] Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor] Great. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that's it.

[doctor] Alright, I'll see you again in a couple of months. We'll reassess your condition and adjust your treatment plan as needed. Take care and follow the post-procedure instructions.

[patient] Thank you, doctor. I'll take care.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care, and we'll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
