dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, thanks. I'm here for my follow-up appointment.

[doctor]: Great, let's go over your medical history. You've been diagnosed with perioral dermatitis, and you also have a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. How has your perioral dermatitis been doing?

[patient]: It's still there, but it's not as bad as it was before. I've been applying the doxycycline twice a day like you told me, but my pharmacist said I should reduce the frequency to once a day since it's improved. So, I've been using it once a day for about a week now.

[doctor]: That's good to hear that you've seen some improvement. Have you noticed any changes in your symptoms since reducing the frequency of the doxycycline?

[patient]: No, it hasn't gotten any better, but it also hasn't gotten any worse. It's just sort of plateaued.

[doctor]: I see. You're still using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day, and you're not experiencing any drying of the skin?

[patient]: No, my skin is actually much improved in terms of moisturization.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. You're also applying the MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash, correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: Are you using any other lotions, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes?

[patient]: No, I'm not using any of those.

[doctor]: You mentioned you didn't start using Elidel because it's not covered by your insurance. Did you consider it because of concerns about potential side effects?

[patient]: Yes, I was worried about the side effects from Elidel.

[doctor]: Understood. Let's take a look at your skin. I'll do a skin inspection. (pauses) On your bilateral medial cheeks, I see a few very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Your Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.

[patient]: Okay, I see.

[doctor]: Based on your current condition, I'm going to recommend that you resume applying the doxycycline cream twice a day until your symptoms fully resolve. Once you've reached that point, you can reduce the application to once a day for an additional week.

[patient]: Alright, I can do that.

[doctor]: I'll also prescribe Tacrolimus for you. This medication can help with your rosacea. I'll review the potential side effects with you before you start.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor]: You'll continue using the Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. I also recommend using a Bruder mask as needed.

[patient]: Okay, got it.

[doctor]: For your ocular rosacea, you'll continue using the MetroCream twice daily. It's also important to gently wash your eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.

[patient]: Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor]: If your symptoms worsen, be sure to call the clinic. You'll have a follow-up appointment in four weeks.

[patient]: Great, I'll make sure to do that. Thank you.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and see you in four weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
