dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm okay, thanks. I've been having some issues with my back lately.

[doctor]: I'm Dr. Johnson. I see you're here for an evaluation of your low back pain. Can you tell me more about it?

[patient]: Sure. It started about 4 months ago when I was helping my son move out of his college dorm. I was lifting some heavy boxes and I felt this sharp pain in my lower back. It was really uncomfortable.

[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds quite painful. Did it resolve on its own or did it persist?

[patient]: It took about an hour to resolve, but after that, I felt like I couldn’t support my upper body weight anymore. It was like my legs were weak.

[doctor]: I see. Did you notice any other symptoms like tingling or numbness in your legs?

[patient]: Yes, I had some tingling in my right toes, but it went away after about an hour.

[doctor]: Alright, and how has the pain been since then?

[patient]: It’s still there. It hurts when I turn a certain way or roll over in my sleep. It’s sharp and uncomfortable.

[doctor]: I understand. Have you tried any treatments for the pain so far?

[patient]: Yes, I’ve been using Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice. The ibuprofen is a bit more helpful than the Tylenol, but it doesn’t really relieve my symptoms.

[doctor]: Hmm, okay. Have you noticed any stomach upset with the ibuprofen?

[patient]: No, I haven’t had any stomach issues.

[doctor]: That’s good to hear. Did you try physical therapy at your gym?

[patient]: Yes, I did. It didn’t help at all.

[doctor]: Alright, have you had any other symptoms like bladder or bowel issues, fevers, or chills?

[patient]: No, none of those.

[doctor]: Okay. Let’s go over your medical history. You had a total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago. How’s your knee been since the surgery?

[patient]: It’s been great. I’d say it’s 95% improved.

[doctor]: That’s excellent to hear. Now, let’s go over your vital signs. Your temperature is normal, blood pressure is 117/56 mm Hg, and your heart rate is 68 BPM. Your oxygen levels are 95% on room air.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: Let’s do a physical examination. I’ll start by checking your neck. I see a bony protuberance of the C5-C6. It doesn’t seem to be painful to the touch. Now, I’ll check your lower back. There’s no pain to palpation, but your flexion and extension are decreased. You also have a positive straight leg raise, and there’s spasm of the paraspinal muscles in your lumbar spine. Your strength is equal bilaterally.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I’ve reviewed your X-rays of the lumbar spine, and they didn’t show any acute bony abnormalities.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: Based on your examination and the X-rays, I think it’s possible that you might have an acute disc herniation. I’m going to order an MRI to evaluate this further.

[patient]: Okay, what’s the next step?

[doctor]: I’ve prescribed meloxicam 15 mg to help with your pain. I also recommend you continue with physical therapy. I’ll provide a referral for that today.

[patient]: Alright, thank you.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns about the plan?

[patient]: No, that sounds good. I’ll follow through with the MRI and physical therapy.

[doctor]: Great. We’ll schedule the MRI and follow up with you once the results are available. If you have any issues or concerns before then, feel free to reach out to us.

[patient]: Thank you, I will. }}<|eot_id|>
