[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Now, let's do a physical exam. How is your neck? Any swelling or tenderness?

[patient]: No, it feels fine.

[doctor]: Good. How about your respiratory system? Any coughing, wheezing, or difficulty breathing?

[patient]: I have shortness of breath and I cough up blood.

[doctor]: Okay. Let's listen to your lungs. I hear some rales and rhonchi, more on the right side. There's also some diminished breath sounds on the right. Do you feel any wheezing now?

[patient]: No, I don't feel any wheezing.

[doctor]: How about your heart? Any palpitations, irregular heartbeat, or any other issues?

[patient]: No, my heart feels normal.

[doctor]: Good. Let's check your abdomen. It feels soft, no tenderness or swelling.

[patient]: That's correct.

[doctor]: Now, let's check your joints. Any pain or swelling in your knees?

[patient]: Yes, I have knee pain.

[doctor]: And any swelling in your lower extremities?

[patient]: Yes, there's some swelling in my legs.

[doctor]: Okay, I see some 1+ non-pitting edema on both lower extremities. Any calf tenderness?

[patient]: No, no calf tenderness.

[doctor]: Any history of bruising?

[patient]: No, no bruising.

[doctor]: Alright, let's summarize what we've discussed. You're experiencing shortness of breath and coughing up blood. You've had a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. You take metformin and Norvasc.

[patient]: Yes, that's correct.

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms, we need to do some further testing to determine the cause. I've ordered a chest x-ray, CT scan, and blood work to be done in the ER. I want you to go to the ER immediately after this appointment, and I'll call ahead to let them know you're coming.

[patient]: Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor]: I've also noticed that your blood sugar hasn't been well controlled. I've ordered an updated A1c today. We'll increase your metformin to 750 mg twice daily.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: Your blood pressure has been running low for about three weeks. I think this could be related to your shortness of breath. Before we pursue further treatment for your hypertension, let's get a better understanding of the cause of your shortness of breath.

[patient]: Okay, that makes sense.

[doctor]: I've instructed you to discontinue the use of Norvasc.

[patient]: Alright, I'll stop taking it.

[doctor]: We'll have you follow up in about three weeks to discuss your diabetes and further treatment recommendations. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor]: Great. Take care and get to the ER right away. We'll see you in three weeks.

[patient]: Thank you, I will. }}<|eot_id|>
