dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi, how are you today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay. I've been having some issues with my right knee.

[doctor] Ah, sorry to hear that. What seems to be going on with your knee?

[patient] I hurt it playing soccer three days ago. I felt a pop in my knee and it started swelling.

[doctor] Oh no, that sounds quite painful. How has the swelling been since then?

[patient] It's improved, but my knee isn't as functional or moving as well as it used to be. It's still swollen, but not as bad as it was initially.

[doctor] I see. How would you rate the pain level on a scale of 1 to 10?

[patient] It's around a 6 or 7 right now. When the swelling goes down, it's more like a 4 or 5.

[doctor] That's helpful to know. Have you been taking any medication for the pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen regularly. It helps a bit.

[doctor] Okay, good. How about your knee movement? Can you bend it at all?

[patient] I can bend it, but it hurts towards the end of the motion. When I try to straighten it, I feel like I need to help it straighten.

[doctor] Hmm, that doesn't sound comfortable. Can you bear weight on your right knee at all?

[patient] Yes, I can, but it feels loose and doesn't feel normal.

[doctor] Alright, let's go through your medical history. Do you work at a desk?

[patient] Yes, I do.

[doctor] Okay. How about any other symptoms or issues besides the knee pain?

[patient] No, just the knee pain.

[doctor] Alright. Let's go through a quick physical exam. Can you walk for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] I notice you're limping slightly on the right leg. Can you show me your right knee?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] There's some swelling, and I see some ecchymosis. When I press on the joint line, it hurts. It's also difficult to move your knee through its range of motion. You can't fully straighten it or bend it as much as you should be able to. Do you feel any pain with this?

[patient] Yes, it hurts.

[doctor] Okay. Let's check your knee further. Your muscle strength seems to be normal, but I'm concerned about the ACL. I'm going to do a Lachman's test. This might be a bit uncomfortable, but it's a quick test. Can you let me do it?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] Alright. The test is positive, which suggests there might be a tear in the ACL. I'm going to order an MRI to confirm this. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it's possible that you have an anterior cruciate ligament tear.

[patient] Oh no, is it serious?

[doctor] It's a significant injury, but we can manage it. I'm going to prescribe you meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce the swelling and pain. We'll also discuss some lifestyle changes to help with the pain and swelling. I recommend using ice and modifying your activities to reduce the swelling.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] I also recommend wearing a knee immobilizer and using crutches when you walk. This will help stabilize your knee and reduce the discomfort. Avoid jogging until we get the results of the MRI.

[patient] Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor] Great. We'll schedule a follow-up appointment once the MRI results are in. We'll review them together and discuss the next steps.

[patient] Okay, sounds good. When will I need to come back?

[doctor] We'll schedule an appointment once the MRI results are available, usually within a week or so. We'll go over them together and discuss the best course of action.

[patient] Alright, I'll wait to hear from you.

[doctor] Great. Take care and follow the recommendations I gave you. We'll get you back to your normal activities as soon as possible.

[patient] Thank you, I appreciate it.

[doctor] You're welcome. See you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
