dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, thanks. I'm here for the evaluation of my right elbow pain.

[doctor]: Ah, right elbow pain. Can you tell me a bit more about that? How long have you been experiencing it?

[patient]: It started about 4 days ago. It's been really bad.

[doctor]: Has it been constant or does it come and go?

[patient]: It's constant. I'm unable to work with my pottery wheel because of the pain.

[doctor]: I see. And have you noticed any specific injury or incident that might have caused the pain?

[patient]: No, I haven't. I've just been using the pottery wheel a lot lately to make a bowl for my son.

[doctor]: Ah, that sounds like a nice project. Does the pain radiate anywhere else from the elbow?

[patient]: Yes, it goes down my arm, medially.

[doctor]: Okay, that's helpful to know. How would you rate the pain on a scale of 1 to 10?

[patient]: It's about a 7, at its worst.

[doctor]: I see. Does the pain wake you up at night?

[patient]: Yes, if I roll over.

[doctor]: Hmm, that's not ideal. Have you tried using ibuprofen or applying ice to see if it helps?

[patient]: No, I haven't.

[doctor]: Alright. Have you had any previous elbow injuries?

[patient]: No, none that I can think of.

[doctor]: Okay, let's talk a bit about your medical history. You have hypertension, is that correct?

[patient]: Yes, I do.

[doctor]: And how have you been managing that?

[patient]: I've been checking my blood pressures regularly. They've been in the 110s to 130s range.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. Are you taking any medications for your hypertension?

[patient]: Yes, I take lisinopril 10 mg daily. I haven't missed any doses.

[doctor]: Excellent. How about your diabetes type 2? How are you managing that?

[patient]: I take metformin 500 mg twice daily, but sometimes I forget to take it.

[doctor]: Hmm, it's important to take your medications regularly. Can you tell me a bit more about your diabetes? How do you manage it?

[patient]: I try to walk 1 to 2 miles each day in the morning and evening. I also do some evening walks around my neighborhood.

[doctor]: That's great. Do you indulge in any sugary foods or drinks?

[patient]: Yes, I have a sweet tooth. I bake cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.

[doctor]: Okay. Let's review your medical history again. You have hypertension and diabetes type 2. You're taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily. Is that correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: Great. Now, let's go through a review of systems. How have you been feeling in terms of any other symptoms?

[patient]: Just the elbow pain, really.

[doctor]: Okay. Any other issues with your joints or muscles?

[patient]: No, just the elbow.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's check your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, your pulse rate is appropriate, respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, oxygen saturation is 100% on room air, and your body temperature is 97.9 degrees F. How's your pain level right now?

[patient]: It's still a 7.

[doctor]: I see. Let's do a quick physical exam. We'll start with your cardiovascular system. Do you hear any murmurs, gallops, or rubs?

[patient]: No, I don't.

[doctor]: Great. Your pulses are equal bilaterally. Now, let's check your respiratory system. Is your breathing normal? Do you have any respiratory distress?

[patient]: No, my breathing is fine.

[doctor]: Excellent. Your lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation. Let's move on to your skin. Do you have any rashes or lesions?

[patient]: No, none.

[doctor]: Okay. Now, let's examine your musculoskeletal system. Specifically, we'll look at your right elbow. Does it hurt when I press on it?

[patient]: Yes, it does.

[doctor]: Okay. There's moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. When you resist pronation of your right forearm, does it hurt?

[patient]: Yes, it does.

[doctor]: And when you flex and bend your right wrist, does it hurt?

[patient]: Yes, it does. But there's no bruising or swelling.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's review the results of your x-ray. The x-ray shows no evidence of any fractures or other abnormalities.

[patient]: Okay, that's good to hear.

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the exam, it looks like you have medial epicondylitis, which is an inflammation of the tendons in your elbow. I'm going to recommend a conservative treatment approach.

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: We'll have you wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide additional support. You'll also apply ice to the area for 20 minutes, three times a day. For pain relief, you'll take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for the next week.

[patient]: Alright, I can do that.

[doctor]: Great. I also advise you to avoid using the pottery wheel for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal. If your pain persists, we may consider formal physical therapy at your next visit.

[patient]: Okay, I understand.

[doctor]: Additionally, I want to discuss your diabetes. You mentioned you sometimes forget to take your metformin. It's crucial to take your medication regularly. I'm going to increase your metformin to 1000 mg twice daily. I'll also refer you to diabetic counseling for more information on managing your diabetes.

[patient]: Okay, I'll make sure to take my medication regularly.

[doctor]: Great. Your hypertension is currently controlled with your medication and exercise. Continue taking lisinopril 10 mg daily. Keep up your walking routine.

[patient]: I will.

[doctor]: We'll schedule a follow-up in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress.

[patient]: Okay, I'll be back in 2 weeks.

[doctor]: All right, thank you for coming in today. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think you've covered everything.

[doctor]: Alright, we'll see you in 2 weeks. Take care and follow the instructions I've given you.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. I'll take care.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
