dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hello, how are you today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay, thanks. I've been having some issues with my knee.

[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about what's been going on with your knee?

[patient] Sure. It started about two and a half weeks ago. I stepped off a curb and twisted my ankle, and ever since then I've had pain on the outside of my right knee.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds like it could be quite uncomfortable. How would you describe the pain?

[patient] It's a dull ache, but it's constant. I can feel it when I'm standing, and it gets worse when I stand on one foot for a while.

[doctor] I see. Do you experience any pain when you're walking or moving around?

[patient] No, not really. It's just when I stand on one foot or stand for a long time.

[doctor] Okay, that's good to know. Do you feel any pain when you're resting or elevating your leg?

[patient] No, my knee feels fine when I'm sitting or lying down.

[doctor] Alright. How about when you're standing with your foot flat on the ground or on your heel?

[patient] Yes, I feel pain when I'm standing on my heel.

[doctor] Hmm, that's interesting. Do you notice any clicking or catching in your knee when you move it?

[patient] Yes, I do hear a clicking sound when I walk.

[doctor] Okay, let's talk about your activity level. How has your knee pain affected your daily activities?

[patient] It hasn't really affected my daily activities too much. I'm more active during the day, but I can still do most things without too much discomfort.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Can you perform a full squat without any pain?

[patient] Yes, I can do a full squat without any pain.

[doctor] Great. Have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your foot?

[patient] Yes, I've had numbness and tingling in my right foot, but it's not new. It's been going on for a while.

[doctor] I see. You mentioned you've been taking ibuprofen for your knee pain. How has that been helping?

[patient] It helps at night, but the pain comes back in the morning.

[doctor] And you've also been icing your knee, right?

[patient] Yes, I've been icing it four times a day, which helps in the morning and at the end of the day.

[doctor] That's good. Let me go over your medical history. Have you had any other injuries or conditions that might be related to your current symptoms?

[patient] Yes, I've fractured my second toe three times over the last 10 years.

[doctor] Okay, I'll keep that in mind. Do you have any other medical conditions or take any medications regularly?

[patient] No, nothing else.

[doctor] Great. How about your job and leisure activities? What do you do for work?

[patient] I'm in sales.

[doctor] Okay. Do you have any regular exercise routines?

[patient] Yes, I ride a bike for exercise.

[doctor] Alright. Let's do a quick review of your systems. How about your neurological symptoms?

[patient] My right foot has been numb and tingling, but it's not new.

[doctor] I see. Any other symptoms or issues?

[patient] No, nothing else.

[doctor] Okay, let's do a physical exam. Can you please lie down and let me check your knee?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] I'll start by checking your circulation. Can you feel this?

[patient] Yes, I can feel it.

[doctor] Great. Now, I'll check your knee. There's some swelling and fluid in your knee, but it doesn't seem to be painful when I touch it.

[patient] That's correct.

[doctor] I'll also do a varus test. This might be a little uncomfortable.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] (performs the varus test) Hmm, that test is positive. That means your lateral collateral ligament is strained.

[patient] What does that mean?

[doctor] It means the ligament on the outside of your knee is stretched or torn. We'll need to get some X-rays to confirm, but it's likely that you have a lateral collateral ligament strain.

[patient] Okay, what do I need to do next?

[doctor] I've ordered X-rays of your knee. We'll need to get those done. In the meantime, I'm going to prescribe meloxicam 15 mg to help with your pain.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good.

[doctor] I'll also recommend formal physical therapy to strengthen your knee. If your pain doesn't improve with physical therapy, we might need to consider an MRI to get a better look at the extent of the damage.

[patient] Okay, I'll follow up with physical therapy. Thank you for your help.

[doctor] You're welcome. We'll get the X-rays done and go from there. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that's all for now.

[doctor] Alright. I'll see you for a follow-up in a few weeks to check on your progress. Take care.

[patient] Thank you, doctor. I will. }}<|eot_id|>
