dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm okay. I've been having some issues with my knees.

[doctor]: Oh, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about what's been going on with your knees?

[patient]: Sure. I've had bilateral knee pain for about 6 months now. It started without any specific injury.

[doctor]: I see. How would you describe the pain?

[patient]: It's a deep, achy pain. It's mostly on the back of my patellae.

[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Are there any swelling or redness in your knees?

[patient]: No, there's no swelling or redness. It's just the pain.

[doctor]: That's helpful to know. How does the pain affect your daily activities?

[patient]: It's really bad when I'm transitioning from a seated position to standing. It's also painful when I'm going up or down stairs.

[doctor]: I understand. Does the pain improve if you rest?

[patient]: Yes, it does. Icing and wrapping my knees with an ACE bandage helps a bit. I also take ibuprofen or Aleve when the pain gets really bad.

[doctor]: Okay, that's good to know. Do you have any other medical issues or previous injuries that might be related to your knee pain?

[patient]: I had a fracture in my upper extremity when I was in 6th grade.

[doctor]: Alright. How often do you exercise and what type of exercise do you do?

[patient]: I usually run outdoors. I try to run 5 to 6 miles per day, but I haven't been able to go that far lately because of the pain. The last time I tried to run 0.5 miles, it was really painful.

[doctor]: I see. Do you have any other activities you enjoy besides running?

[patient]: I work from home, so I don't have to commute.

[doctor]: That's good. Let's go through some other areas to see if there's anything else going on. How about your heart and lungs? Any issues there?

[patient]: No, nothing.

[doctor]: Okay. Any other symptoms like fever, dizziness, or nausea?

[patient]: No, none of those.

[doctor]: Alright. Now, let's do a physical exam. Can you stand up and walk for me?

[patient]: Sure.

[doctor]: I see your gait is normal, no deformity. Let's check your heart. Your heart sounds normal, no murmurs. Any swelling in your legs?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: How about your reflexes? Your patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical, good.

[doctor]: Now, let's examine your knees. There's no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. No effusion. You have tenderness to palpation of the retinaculum bilaterally. I also do a patellar grind test, and you have pain with squatting. But your Lachman test, anterior and posterior drawer tests, and McMurray test are all negative bilaterally.

[patient]: What does it all mean?

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the exam, it looks like you have bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: It's a common condition where the pain is in the front of your knee, around the patella. The good news is that it's treatable. We'll start with conservative treatment, which includes formal physical therapy to increase your lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. We'll also encourage you to take it easy on your running for now. You can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good. I'll start physical therapy.

[doctor]: Great. We'll review your X-rays, which show no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis. It's a good sign that there's no structural damage.

[patient]: That's a relief.

[doctor]: Yes, it is. You'll follow up with me soon, and we'll re-evaluate your progress. How does that sound?

[patient]: Sounds good.

[doctor]: Alright, any other questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think that's it.

[doctor]: Great. We'll see you soon then. Take care.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
