dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Good morning, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Good, thank you. I'm here for my appointment.

[doctor]: Great, let's get started. Can you tell me a little bit about what brings you in today?

[patient]: Yes, I've been having a lot of pain in my left knee. It started a week ago when I was running around with my niece.

[doctor]: Ah, I see. What happened exactly?

[patient]: I fell and heard a pop. I think I must have twisted my knee.

[doctor]: That sounds like it could have been a pretty significant injury. How has your knee been feeling since then?

[patient]: It's been really painful. I've been limping around. The pain is on the inside and outside of my knee.

[doctor]: That sounds uncomfortable. On a scale of 1 to 10, how would you rate the pain?

[patient]: It's a 4.

[doctor]: Okay, and how would you describe the pain? Is it sharp, dull, throbbing, or something else?

[patient]: It's throbbing.

[doctor]: I see. Have you been trying any treatments to help with the pain?

[patient]: Yes, I've been taking Tylenol and wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape. The Tylenol has given me some relief, but it's not lasting long.

[doctor]: That's helpful. Are you able to take ibuprofen at all?

[patient]: No, I can't. I have an ulcer.

[doctor]: Okay, I'll keep that in mind. Let's take a look at your knee. Can you stand up and let me examine it?

[patient]: Sure.

[doctor]: Great. I'll start by checking for any swelling. Do you notice any swelling around your knee?

[patient]: Yes, I can see some swelling.

[doctor]: Okay. Now, let's check your range of motion. Can you bend your knee for me?

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: And how does that feel?

[patient]: It hurts.

[doctor]: Alright, and can you straighten your knee for me?

[patient]: Yeah.

[doctor]: And how does that feel?

[patient]: It hurts too.

[doctor]: I see. Let's check for any instability. Can you do a straight leg raise for me?

[patient]: Sure.

[doctor]: Great. And how does your knee feel when you do that?

[patient]: It hurts a bit.

[doctor]: Okay. We'll also do a valgus and varus stress test. Can you let me push your knee in and out for me?

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Great. How does that feel?

[patient]: It doesn't feel any worse.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's take some x-rays of your knee to get a better look at what might be going on. We'll take four views: anteroposterior, lateral, skyline, and posteroanterior.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: We'll get those developed and review them together. Based on the exam, it's possible that you may have a mild MCL strain. We'll need to wait for the x-ray results to confirm.

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: The MCL, or medial collateral ligament, is a ligament on the inside of your knee that connects your femur to your tibia. If it's strained, it can cause pain and swelling on the inside of the knee. The x-rays will help us confirm whether that's the case.

[patient]: Okay, I see.

[doctor]: We'll also need to immobilize your knee to help it heal. I'm going to prescribe a knee immobilizer for you to wear for the next week. This will help reduce the movement and give your knee a chance to rest.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: I'll also prescribe tramadol 50 mg to help manage your pain. You can take this as directed on the bottle, and it should help with your discomfort. Additionally, you can continue to use Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor]: It's also important to ice your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. This will help reduce the inflammation and pain.

[patient]: I can do that.

[doctor]: Finally, I'm going to advise you to avoid running for the next 3 weeks. This will give your knee a chance to heal and prevent further injury.

[patient]: Okay, that's not too bad. I can do that.

[doctor]: If your pain doesn't improve with the immobilizer, we'll need to consider an MRI to get a better look at what's going on inside your knee.

[patient]: Alright, I understand.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?

[patient]: No, I think you've covered everything.

[doctor]: Great. I'll get the x-rays developed and we'll review them together. If you have any questions or concerns, feel free to call the office. We'll see you back in 2 weeks for a follow-up.

[patient]: Thank you.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and we'll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
